# Where the MLOps tools become useful

The key concept is:

> **Don't use a tool just because it's an MLOps tool. Use it when its abstraction solves a problem you actually have.**

For example:

### Your own implementation

```text
Python
 ├── data.py
 ├── features.py
 ├── train.py
 ├── evaluate.py
 ├── model.py
 └── pipeline.py
```

Great for understanding the system.

Then you might eventually replace:

```text
pipeline.py
```

with:

```text
Airflow / Prefect / Dagster
```

while keeping:

```text
data.py
features.py
train.py
evaluate.py
model.py
```

The orchestration tool isn't replacing your ML engineering.

It's replacing the **workflow-management infrastructure** around your code.

